1	#!/usr/bin/env node
2	// The sheepdog pack's FORCE-BASELINE sweep — the enforcer's one manual lever over the
3	// whole fleet. Run by the pack's `fleet-baseline` workflow (stubs/workflows/fleet-baseline.yml,
4	// vendored into the enforcer repo's own .github/ by the `sheepdog-fleet-baseline` migration),
5	// on `workflow_dispatch` only. Still runnable by hand (`node force-fleet-baseline.mjs`) via
6	// the CLI guard at the foot.
7	//
8	// WHAT IT DOES. Enumerate every covered member under the configured owner and, for each,
9	// fire ITS OWN scheduler workflow with `overrides: FORCE_TASKS=baselining` — the same
10	// manual-run lever the owner would pull by hand in that repo's Actions tab, pulled across
11	// the fleet in one run. Nothing here re-implements baselining: each member converges its
12	// own mount, with its own token, under its own scheduler and its own delivery policy. This
13	// sweep only presses the button, which is why it is a dispatcher and not a maintainer.
14	//
15	// AND THEN IT WATCHES. Dispatching is half the job: a 204 means QUEUED, and the owner
16	// pulls this lever exactly on the occasions they are standing by for the RESULT. So the
17	// run does not end at the last dispatch — it follows every member it forced until that
18	// member reaches a terminal state (its maintenance PR merged, or nothing to deliver, or a
19	// PR left open for a `review` member to merge), including the nights where the converge
20	// hands off to an AGENT that runs outside the member's Actions run entirely. That half
21	// lives in follow-fleet-baseline.mjs, beside this file: what it observes, when a member
22	// counts as finished, and the report it renders (updated members with their OLD and new
23	// canon refs, lines changed, per-member timing, errors and warnings, whether an agent was
24	// involved). Set the `follow` input false for the old fire-and-forget behaviour.
25	//
26	// WHY IT EXISTS. Under per-project scheduling every member baselines itself hourly, so the
27	// fleet needs no push in the ordinary case. The cases it is FOR are the un-ordinary ones:
28	// a canon change the fleet should pick up now rather than over the next day (a broken
29	// vendored file, a migration record that must land before something else), and the tail of
30	// members whose next slot is hours away when someone is standing by to watch the result.
31	// A forced run bypasses baselining's precondition (the engine records it as forced and says
32	// the precondition was never evaluated), so a member with nothing to do converges to a
33	// cheap no-op — the sweep is safe to over-use, only wasteful.
34	//
35	// WHY IT IS A WORKFLOW AND NOT A TASK. Every other thing this pack does is a scheduled
36	// task, because it answers a recurring question. This answers no question and has no
37	// cadence: it is an OWNER-INITIATED operation with its own inputs (a repo filter, a dry
38	// run), and the thing that starts it is a human pressing "Run workflow". It carries no
39	// `schedule:` trigger, so the doctrine it would otherwise break — a repo's vendored
40	// scheduler is its ONLY cron (packs/basics/scheduled-tasks.md) — is untouched.
41	//
42	// THE TOKEN. FLEET_GITHUB_TOKEN, the same account-spanning PAT the three sweeps use, plus
43	// ONE scope they do not need: **Actions: read and write** on the owner's repositories.
44	// Dispatching another repo's workflow is a write to that repo's Actions, and a PAT scoped
45	// only for the sweeps gets a 403 here. That 403 is reported per repo and fails the run
46	// rather than being retried, because it is a grant to fix once, not a transient.
47	//
48	// Dependency-free (global fetch, Node 20+). It writes NOTHING to any member — no commit,
49	// no issue, no comment; the single side effect per member is one queued Actions run.
50	//
51	// It lives in its own folder under the pack root, beside the tasks rather than inside one,
52	// because it belongs to no task — its runner is the vendored workflow. What it shares with
53	// the sweeps stays where it was: the REST primitives (fleet-api.mjs) and the config reader
54	// (fleet-config.mjs), both one level up.
55	
56	import { appendFileSync } from 'node:fs';
57	import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
58	import { makeGh, paged, readDeclaration, isDormant, DECLARATION } from '../fleet-api.mjs';
59	import { parseSheepdogConfig } from '../fleet-config.mjs';
60	import {
61	  latestRunId, followFleet, renderFollowReport, deliveryOf, stampedRef,
62	  parseFollowMinutes, asBool, DEFAULT_FOLLOW_MINUTES,
63	} from './follow-fleet-baseline.mjs';
64	
65	// The member-side workflow being fired, and the exact manual-run override it is fired
66	// with. Both are the vendored scheduler's public surface (engine/scheduler/stubs/
67	// claudinite-scheduler.yml + `forcedTaskIds` in engine/scheduler/run.mjs); named here as
68	// constants so the coupling is one line to find, not a string buried in a request body.
69	export const SCHEDULER = 'claudinite-scheduler.yml';
70	export const FORCE_OVERRIDES = 'FORCE_TASKS=baselining';
71	
72	// --- classification (pure) ----------------------------------------------------
73	
74	// What the dispatch POST's status means. Every branch is a DIFFERENT thing for the
75	// reader to do, which is why the sweep reports a state per repo instead of a count of
76	// failures: a 404 is a repo to adopt, a 403 is a token to re-scope, a 422 is a workflow
77	// GitHub has switched off. Kept free of I/O so the whole table is testable directly.
78	export function classifyDispatch(status) {
79	  switch (status) {
80	    case 204:
81	      return { state: 'forced', detail: 'baselining queued on its own scheduler' };
82	    case 404:
83	      // The workflow is missing, the repo has Actions disabled, or the PAT cannot see the
84	      // repo at all — indistinguishable from here, and all three mean "nothing was queued".
85	      return { state: 'no-scheduler', detail: `no ${SCHEDULER} on the default branch, or Actions is disabled — nothing there can baseline itself` };
86	    case 403:
87	      return { state: 'no-permission', detail: 'the PAT lacks Actions: write on this repo — dispatching a workflow is an Actions write, a scope the read-only sweeps never needed' };
88	    case 422:
89	      // GitHub disables a repo's scheduled workflows after 60 days of inactivity, and a
90	      // disabled workflow refuses dispatch; a scheduler stub too old to carry the
91	      // `overrides` input lands here too.
92	      return { state: 'not-dispatchable', detail: 'the workflow exists but refused the dispatch — it is disabled (GitHub switches off cron on inactive repos), or too old to accept the `overrides` input' };
93	    default:
94	      return { state: 'error', detail: `dispatch returned ${status}` };
95	  }
96	}
97	
98	// The `repos` input, resolved against the owner: a comma/whitespace-separated list of
99	// bare names or full `owner/name`, lowercased, or null for "every member". A bare name is
100	// qualified with the configured owner, because that is the only owner this sweep can
101	// reach and typing it twenty times is friction with no upside.
102	export function parseRepoFilter(raw, owner) {
103	  const names = String(raw ?? '').split(/[\s,]+/).map((s) => s.trim()).filter(Boolean);
104	  if (!names.length) return null;
105	  return new Set(names.map((n) => (n.includes('/') ? n : `${owner}/${n}`).toLowerCase()));
106	}
107	
108	// --- the dispatch -------------------------------------------------------------
109	
110	// Fire one member's scheduler. `ref` is the member's DEFAULT branch (read from the
111	// enumeration, never assumed to be `main`): workflow_dispatch resolves the workflow file
112	// on the ref it is given, and a repo whose trunk is `master` would otherwise 404 as if it
113	// carried no scheduler at all.
114	export async function forceOne(gh, fullName, ref) {
